<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malik ibn Anas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 711–<a href="/years/795">795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Mālik ibn ʾAnas, Imam of Medina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malik ibn Anas was a Muslim scholar, jurist and traditionalist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, the eponym of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a>, one of the four schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> jurisprudence. Born in Medina around 711, he studied under Hisham ibn Urwa, Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri and Ja'far al-Sadiq and became the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> scholar of his day. His al-Muwatta', composed in the early Abbasid period, is one of the oldest Sunni hadith collections and law books. Accounts hold that he refused a wish of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur to impose it on the whole Islamic world. He died in Medina in 795 and was buried in the cemetery of Al-Baqi'; his school became the norm in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malik-ibn-anas.jpg" alt="Malik ibn Anas" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sun'i · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chester_Beatty_T_414_fol_130r_Mālik.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Malik was the son of Anas ibn Malik, not the Companion of the same name, and of Aaliyah bint Shurayk al-Azdiyya. His family came originally from the al-Asbahi tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>; his great-grandfather Abu '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> moved it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> after converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and his grandfather Malik ibn Abi Amir took part in collecting the Quranic parchments under the caliph Uthman. Malik memorised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> in his youth, learned recitation from Abu Suhail Nafi' ibn 'Abd ar-Rahman, who gave him his ijazah, the permission to teach, and studied under Hisham ibn Urwah, Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri and Ja'far al-Sadiq.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">Hadith</a> scholars including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-bukhari">Muhammad al-Bukhari</a> called the chain Malik, from Nafi' the freed slave of Ibn Umar, from Ibn Umar, from Muhammad, the Golden Chain of Narrators. His al-Muwatta', composed in the early Abbasid period, set out the law, ritual and practice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and aimed to give a &quot;smoothed path&quot;, which is what the title means, through disagreement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shafii">Al-Shafi'i</a> called it the soundest book on earth after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>.</p><p>In theology he opposed anthropomorphism. Asked about the Throne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> 20:5, he answered that the manner is unknown, belief obligatory and asking about it an innovation. He valued differences of opinion among scholars as a mercy. Reports say that the Abbasid caliph al-Mansur proposed to make the Muwatta' law for the whole empire on pain of death for those who differed, and that Malik refused, saying each region should keep the knowledge it had. He is also remembered for saying that the scholar's shield is &quot;I do not know&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Malik died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 795 at the age of 83 or 84 and was buried in the cemetery of Al-Baqi', opposite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Mosque of the Prophet</a>. The Abbasid governor Abdallah al-Zaynabi led the funeral prayers. A small shrine built over his grave in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> was demolished after the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia was established in 1932. His pupil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shafii">al-Shafi'i</a> founded another of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> schools, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> became normative in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malik ibn Anas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
